[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Extension Model Overview vs JUnit 4 Rules/Runners"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Migrating from JUnit 4 to JUnit 5 requires understanding how extensibility has been redesigned. JUnit 4 relied heavily on @RunWith and TestRule implementations, which often suffered from composition limitations. JUnit 5 introduces the Extension Model, a singular, highly flexible mechanism built around extension points."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The JUnit 4 Approach: Runners and Rules"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In JUnit 4, changing how a test ran at the class level required a custom Runner via the @RunWith annotation. Because a test class could only specify a single runner, combining features like Spring integration and parameterized tests was complex and often required custom workarounds."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@RunWith(SpringJUnit4ClassRunner.class)
public class JUnit4ExampleTest {
    @Rule
    public TemporaryFolder folder = new TemporaryFolder();

    @Test
    public void testUsingRule() {
        // Test logic here
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The JUnit 5 Approach: The Extension Model"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit 5 discards the concept of custom runners entirely. Instead, it introduces the @ExtendWith annotation. Multiple extensions can be registered concurrently on a single test class or method, solving the composition problem inherent in JUnit 4 runners."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class)
@ExtendWith(CustomTimingExtension.class)
public class JUnit5ExampleTest {
    @Test
    public void testUsingExtensions() {
        // Test logic here
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Architectural Differences"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single vs Multiple: JUnit 4 allowed only one @RunWith per class. JUnit 5 permits unlimited @ExtendWith declarations."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lifecycle Hooks: JUnit 4 used @Before / @After rules. JUnit 5 uses specific extension interfaces like BeforeAllCallback, BeforeEachCallback, and TestExecutionExceptionHandler."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dependency Injection: JUnit 5 extensions can natively resolve method and constructor parameters using the ParameterResolver interface, a capability lacking in standard JUnit 4 rules."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The JUnit 5 Extension Model provides a cleaner, more composable architecture than JUnit 4 runners and rules. By adopting interface-based extensions and native parameter resolution, developers can write modular and maintainable test infrastructure."}]
